# The QEMU kernel, for a machine that is the phone's part rather than a
# convenient one: qemu-system-arm -M virt -cpu cortex-a7 -smp 1 -m 64.
#
# ============ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ============
#
# The emulator used to be aarch64. That gave it 64-bit time_t, size_t and
# pointers and relaxed alignment, so an entire class of bug -- the class this
# project will actually hit, because the phone is a 32-bit Cortex-A7 with
# 64 MB -- passed every test here and failed on the bench. One ABI on both
# machines moves those failures into the emulator. This config is the kernel
# half of that move; neodct_qemu_defconfig is the userland half.
#
# ============ MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED ============
#
# Built and booted in exactly the machine named above, on 6.12.47:
#
#   uname -m           armv7l
#   CPU part           0xc07 (Cortex-A7), CPU architecture 7
#   features           neon vfpv3 vfpv4 idiva idivt thumbee lpae
#   zImage             4,289,584 bytes
#   MemTotal           53,824 kB of a 64 MB machine, booted with no -dtb
#                      54,812 kB when run_qemu.sh passes one -- see below
#
# The features line is what makes BR2_ARM_FPU_NEON_VFPV4 and Thumb-2 in the
# defconfig exercised rather than aspirational, and MemTotal is the number
# that makes the emulator a fair test: the phone has ~54 MB usable of its
# 64 MB, and this kernel is within a megabyte of that in either direction
# depending on whether a device tree is passed.
#
# WHICH DIRECTION MATTERS AND THIS FILE USED TO GET IT WRONG. It said "a thing
# that fits here fits there" six lines above the block admitting the emulator
# had moved ~988 kB ABOVE the phone -- so the sentence asserting the safe
# direction survived the change that reversed it, which is the branch's own
# failure mode written into its own documentation. The honest statement is the
# other way round: an ordinary session has ~1 MB MORE than the phone, so a
# thing that fits here may still not fit there, and the margin to check
# against is 53,824 kB (the no-dtb number) rather than 54,812.
#
# THE SECOND MemTotal IS THE ONE AN ORDINARY SESSION SEES, and it is ~1 MB
# ABOVE the phone rather than below it. run_qemu.sh now hands QEMU a device
# tree -- see the device-tree block below -- and the kernel reserves
# fdt_totalsize() out of the guest. QEMU's own generated blob is padded to
# 1 MiB and a dtc-produced one is 8 KB, so passing a smaller tree gives the
# reservation back and MemTotal RISES. Measured on this kernel, same rootfs:
#
#   no -dtb                                MemTotal 53,824 kB
#   -dtb, the merged 8,270-byte tree       MemTotal 54,812 kB
#
# 988 kB, and it is recorded here rather than hidden because AGENTS.md,
# BUILDING.md and docs/BLUETOOTH.md all quote this number and none of them can
# see it move. A 256 KiB pad (dtc -S 0x40000) recovers half of it and a 1 MiB
# pad does not boot at all -- no console output whatsoever -- so the honest
# answer is to write the new number down, not to reach for a magic pad size.
#
# ============ WHY IT IS BUILT UP AND NOT PORTED ACROSS ============
#
# DECISIONS.md D4. Two rejected starting points, both measured:
#
#   multi_v7_defconfig, trimmed          MemTotal 42,492 kB, zImage 9,019,904 B
#   this fragment, merged onto nothing   MemTotal 53,824 kB, zImage 4,195,616 B
#
# multi_v7 carries ~40 SoCs and disabling them by name does not work -- the
# ARCH_OMAP symbol survived every attempt and mach-omap2 still compiled. A
# kernel that leaves 42 MB of 64 is not parity with a phone that leaves 54; it
# is a divergence in the other direction, 12 MB HARSHER than the hardware,
# which is still a divergence. Porting board/qemu/aarch64-virt/linux.config
# across was the other candidate and carries no I2C, SPI, GPIO, FB_VIRTUAL,
# UBIFS, raw NAND, MMC, PWM or backlight at all -- none of the stacks the
# later stages need.
#
# EVERY SYMBOL ADDED HERE SPENDS SOME OF THAT MemTotal. The number is only
# worth having while it stays measured, so a change to this file means booting
# it again and writing down the new MemTotal, not reasoning about it.
#
# ============ IT IS savedefconfig FORMAT, AND THAT IS LEGITIMATE ============
#
# 86 lines, not the ~3,200 of a whole .config. Buildroot copies
# BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_CONFIG_FILE to .config and then runs olddefconfig
# (package/pkg-kconfig.mk), which fills in every default -- so a minimal
# defconfig is exactly what that path is designed to take. Verified here:
# `make ARCH=arm olddefconfig` followed by `make savedefconfig` round-trips
# this file with zero differences. The body below is that savedefconfig output
# verbatim, in its order -- three lines used to sit in hand-chosen places
# instead, which meant the diff check two paragraphs down did not actually
# pass on the file it describes.
#
# REGENERATING IT DESTROYS THIS HEADER, AND BOTH TARGETS DO. This said to use
# `linux-update-defconfig` and not `linux-update-config`, as though the first
# were comment-safe. It is not: package/pkg-kconfig.mk defines both through
# kconfig-package-update-config, whose body is `cp -f` over
# BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_CONFIG_FILE. The difference between the two targets
# is defconfig format versus an expanded .config, not what happens to the 383
# lines above. Run either and this file becomes the bare 86.
#
# So the procedure is two steps, and the second one is the one people skip:
#
#   1. make linux-update-defconfig
#   2. put this header back on top of the generated lines
#
# `diff <(grep -v '^#' <this file>) $(LINUX_DIR)/defconfig` is the check that
# the body is what the kernel just wrote. What cannot be recovered by hand is
# everything below: the measured MemTotal and zImage numbers, the multi_v7
# comparison, the four traps, the device-tree block, the absence list, and the
# NAMESPACES record that trap 4 explains cannot live anywhere else. A
# regeneration that eats them shows up in `git diff` as ordinary churn.
#
# ============ AND A CHANGE HERE MEANS RE-RUNNING THE PARITY PROBE =========
#
# This file and run_qemu.sh's machine are the two things that decide what an
# nd-inventory capture of the emulator says, and for a while nothing in the
# tree read either of them: every parity gate checked
# neodct/tests/parity/allow.txt against a committed FILE. So:
#
#   cd neodct/src && make parity-probe ND_KERNEL=<zImage> ND_ROOTFS=<busybox dir>
#
# boots this kernel, takes a fresh capture and requires it to equal
# neodct/tests/parity/qemu-armv7-probe.inventory byte for byte. Four seconds.
# Any symbol added or removed below can move that file, and a symbol change
# that moves it silently is the failure the whole parity directory exists to
# stop -- measured, dropping one cmdline parameter cost the emulator gpio53,
# 56 and 57 with every other gate still green.
#
# ============ THE FOUR TRAPS ITS SYMBOLS EXIST TO AVOID ============
#
# Each of these produced a kernel that boots perfectly and is silently broken.
#
# 1. MD IS THE GATE UNDER THE WHOLE IMMUTABLE-ROOTFS DESIGN. Asking for
#    BLK_DEV_DM and DM_VERITY and running olddefconfig DROPPED BOTH, because
#    BLK_DEV_DM depends on MD and MD was not set. The kernel booted with no
#    device-mapper whatsoever -- i.e. an image whose verity root cannot work --
#    and nothing in the build said so. So MD is spelled out here even though
#    nothing reads it directly. (And the check afterwards is
#    /dev/mapper/control, not `grep verity /proc/devices`: verity is a DM
#    target, not a device, so the obvious check reports failure on a working
#    kernel.)
#
# 2. THE FRAMEBUFFER NEEDS `video=vfb:on`, NOT `vfb.vfb_enable=1`. With
#    FB_VIRTUAL built in, the module parameter is useless: vfb_init() calls
#    vfb_setup(option) BEFORE testing the flag, and vfb_setup() opens by
#    assigning vfb_enable = 0, overwriting whatever module_param just parsed.
#    It only sets it back on a non-empty option string. So the parameter form
#    produces no /dev/fb0 and no line in dmesg, which looks exactly like
#    "FB_VIRTUAL does not work". Any non-empty option works; `video=vfb:on` is
#    the one to use, and userspace sets the mode afterwards with
#    FBIOPUT_VSCREENINFO -- which is what neodctDisplay.c's force_mode() has
#    always done on the phone, so the geometry does not belong on the cmdline
#    at all. FB_MODE_HELPERS is deliberately absent for that reason: it buys
#    nothing here and costs kernel text on a 64 MB machine.
#
# 3. MTDRAM IS BUILT IN, unlike the aarch64 config where it is a module. A
#    module is a dead end here: this image boots an initramfs and installs no
#    /lib/modules, so modprobe has nothing to load. Note also that mtdram
#    reports write=1, so every LEB size and VID header offset computed on it
#    is arithmetic the phone never does -- NANDSIM is the device that
#    reproduces the Pico Mini's part exactly (128 MB, 128 KiB PEB, 2048-byte
#    page, 64-byte OOB) and allocates lazily, so it costs no guest RAM. Both
#    are here; the harness should use nandsim.
#
#    BUT BUILT IN IS NOT FREE, AND THIS SAID IT WAS. Both instantiate
#    themselves at boot with no parameters at all: mtdram vmallocs its
#    CONFIG_MTDRAM_TOTAL_SIZE default of 4096 KiB out of the guest's 64 MB,
#    and nandsim comes up at its own default 16 KiB PEB / 512-byte page --
#    which is the wrong geometry, shadowing the right one. Measured at -m 64
#    on this kernel, on the cmdline run_qemu.sh builds:
#
#      default                        mtd0 mtdram 4 MiB + mtd1 nandsim
#                                     16 KiB erase, MemFree 37,220 kB
#      mtdram.total_size=0 + the       one device, erase 131072 write 2048,
#      Pico Mini's nandsim ID bytes    MemFree 42,120 kB
#
#    ~4.8 MB of a 64 MB machine, on every ordinary boot, and MemTotal is
#    53,824 kB in both -- so the one number AGENTS.md, BUILDING.md and this
#    header all quote cannot show it. run_qemu.sh's default cmdline therefore
#    carries both parameters, which is where they belong: a kernel symbol that
#    costs nothing when it is given arguments is not the same as one that
#    costs nothing.
#
# 4. CONFIG_EXPERT DELETES THE NAMESPACES. This is trap 1 wearing a different
#    hat and it is the worst of the four, because what it takes away is the
#    confinement the whole security plan stands on.
#
#    GPIO_SYSFS is `bool "/sys/class/gpio/... (sysfs interface)" if EXPERT`
#    with no default, so without EXPERT it has no prompt and olddefconfig
#    silently drops the =y. EXPERT is therefore not a taste; it is the only
#    way to ask for the legacy GPIO interface that nd_backlight.c's GPIO tier,
#    S90display and neodctDisplay.c all drive.
#
#    And init/Kconfig:1271 is `menuconfig NAMESPACES ... default !EXPERT`.
#    Setting EXPERT flips that default to n, so a FRESH expansion of this file
#    -- which is exactly what Buildroot does, `cp` the defconfig over .config
#    and run olddefconfig -- comes out with
#
#        # CONFIG_NAMESPACES is not set
#
#    and takes UTS_NS, IPC_NS, PID_NS, NET_NS and USER_NS with it. CONFIG_USER_NS
#    is listed below and was listed before, and it did NOT survive: it lives
#    inside the NAMESPACES menu, so the gate above it wins. Measured by doing
#    the round trip rather than by adding EXPERT to a populated .config, where
#    olddefconfig keeps the =y that is already there and the trap does not
#    fire at all.
#
#    So CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y is spelled out below even though nothing reads it
#    directly, for the same reason CONFIG_MD is. The block near the end of this
#    header used to say these symbols cannot be spelled out because
#    savedefconfig omits defaults; that stopped being true the moment EXPERT
#    landed, and it is rewritten there rather than left to mislead.
#
#    EXPERT costs three other things, all measured on the round trip and none
#    of them load-bearing here: eleven HID device drivers and INPUT_FF_MEMLESS
#    (they are `default !EXPERT`, they need a HID transport, and this machine
#    has neither USB host nor Bluetooth), DEBUG_MEMORY_INIT (a debug aid), and
#    -- the other way -- DEBUG_KERNEL, which EXPERT `select`s outright to
#    unhide options, which in turn flips the FTRACE *menu* on. No tracer under
#    it is selected and nothing in any Makefile keys on bare CONFIG_FTRACE, so
#    it is a menu symbol and not code. The whole stage together -- five new
#    drivers in, TEST_POWER and NEW_LEDS out, and all of that -- is +24,928
#    bytes of zImage: 4,264,656 -> 4,289,584. Both numbers were built and
#    booted here on the same rootfs.
#
# ============ THE DEVICE TREE, AND THE FOUR SMALL SURFACES ============
#
# CPU_FREQ, CPUFREQ_DT, BACKLIGHT_CLASS_DEVICE and BACKLIGHT_PWM have been in
# this file since it landed and had nothing to bind to. `-M virt`'s cpu@0
# declares neither `clocks` nor `operating-points-v2`, so
# cpufreq_dt_platdev_init() creates no device; the machine has no PWM
# controller at all, so pwm-backlight never probes. Measured on the Stage-1
# kernel: /sys/class/backlight EMPTY and
# /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq -- which is ND_CPUFREQ_DIR, the only
# path nd_cpufreq.c opens -- ABSENT, with both drivers loaded and bound to
# nothing.
#
# The devices come from neodct/board/qemu/nd-virt-additions.dtsi, which
# run_qemu.sh appends to QEMU's own generated tree at run time. That is why
# GPIOLIB, GPIO_SYSFS, GPIO_PL061, GPIO_MOCKUP and PWM_GPIO are here: the
# backlight is a pwm-backlight over a software PWM on a pl061 line, and
# gpio-mockup at `gpio-mockup.gpio_mockup_ranges=0,64` is what makes gpio53,
# gpio56 and gpio57 -- the Mini B's BL, RST and DC pins, hard-coded in
# ND_BL_GPIO_PIN and neodctDisplay.c -- exportable. -M virt's own pl061 is
# eight lines at base 512, so none of the phone's pin numbers exists on it:
# measured, `echo 53 > export` gives "export_store: invalid GPIO 53".
#
# GPIO_SIM is the symbol 6.12 recommends instead, and it cannot be used: it is
# configfs-driven with no fixed gpiobase, so it cannot produce gpio53. That
# gpio-mockup is marked DEPRECATED is a recorded risk, not a reason to ship
# pin numbers that do not match the phone.
#
# THREE SURFACES ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT ABSENT AND TWO SYMBOLS CAME OUT FOR IT.
#
#   CONFIG_TEST_POWER is REMOVED. Nothing in this tree reads power_supply --
#   one grep hit repo-wide and it is prose in docs/SLEEP.md -- and the phone's
#   battery is not a power_supply device at all: it is a MAX1704x fuel gauge
#   on /dev/i2c-3 at 0x36, and nd_battery.c's SIM/LIVE/UNREADABLE gate is
#   decided by errno at open(2) of that node and nothing else. What TEST_POWER
#   put in the image was three supplies named test_ac, test_battery and
#   test_usb -- string literals in drivers/power/supply/test_power.c, settable
#   by no module parameter, so the NAME cannot be made right without patching
#   the kernel -- one of which reports capacity=50, present=1,
#   status=Discharging and voltage_now=3300. A second, contradictory battery
#   in an image whose real battery is simulated is the exact class of lie
#   nd_battery.h's three-source block and ND_MODEM_LINK_ABSENT were written to
#   stop telling. It also cost three "__power_supply_register: Expected proper
#   parent device" warnings on every boot: measured, three before, none after.
#   POWER_SUPPLY stays, so the class directory exists and is empty -- the
#   honest "the subsystem is here, the device is not" state.
#
#   THERMAL stays and has no zone, which is the same removal seen from the
#   other side. power_supply_core.c's psy_register_thermal() registers a zone
#   named after any supply carrying a TEMP property, so QEMU's only thermal
#   zone was typed `test_battery` -- measured, thermal_zone0 type=test_battery
#   temp=2600. Nothing in this tree reads thermal at all, and a wrong-named
#   node in a directory with no readers is worse than an empty directory,
#   because it is the first thing a future consumer would bind to. Measured
#   after the removal: /sys/class/thermal is [].
#
#   CONFIG_NEW_LEDS is REMOVED as dead weight. Nothing reads LEDs -- zero grep
#   hits repo-wide -- and NEW_LEDS on its own creates nothing: LEDS_CLASS is
#   what builds the class, so measured, /sys/class/leds did not exist with the
#   symbol set and does not exist without it.
#
#   The RTC needed nothing. PL031 binds, /dev/rtc0 is there, hctosys=1, and
#   the kernel sets the system clock from it at boot -- and /dev/rtc0 is the
#   only name nd_clock.c uses, CLOCK_RTC_DEVICES being {"/dev/rtc0",
#   "/dev/rtc"}. What the emulator could never reproduce is the condition
#   nd_clock.c's opening sentence names -- "RTC boots at the Unix epoch" --
#   because QEMU's PL031 reads the host's wall clock. `-rtc base=1970-01-01`
#   is that condition exactly (measured: "setting system clock to
#   1970-01-01T00:00:02 UTC"), and run_qemu.sh carries it behind NEODCT_RTC.
#
# ============ WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY YET ============
#
# This is the proven floor, not a feature-equivalent replacement for the
# aarch64 config, and every absence below is silent at runtime -- the image
# boots and the feature is simply not there. Listed so that it is found here
# rather than on the bench:
#
#   networking          no VIRTIO_NET, no NETDEVICES. NEODCT_NET, the browser,
#                       nd_remote's update download and openssh have no device
#                       to bind. Restoring it is two symbols and a re-boot for
#                       a fresh MemTotal.
#   the modem data path no USB_NET_QMI_WWAN / RNDIS_HOST / USBNET, which is
#                       what qemu_modem_data_test.py exercises.
#   Bluetooth           no BT at all, while bluez5, bluez-alsa and nd_bt.c all
#                       still ship in this defconfig. The RTL8761BU reasoning
#                       and the BT_HCIBTUSB_RTL choice are in the aarch64 file.
#   the display         no PCI, PCI_HOST_GENERIC, VIRTIO_PCI, DRM or
#                       DRM_VIRTIO_GPU. Deliberate, and a gain rather than a
#                       loss: virtio-mmio needs no PCI on arm virt (measured --
#                       virtio-blk, -gpu and -keyboard all bound), and vfb is
#                       the phone's own driver. Stage 3. PCI_HOST_GENERIC is
#                       named beside PCI because CONFIG_PCI alone is trap 1
#                       again: measured, `./scripts/config -e PCI` plus
#                       olddefconfig gives PCI=y, USB_PCI=y and USB_XHCI_PCI=y
#                       for free and leaves PCI_HOST_GENERIC off, so -M virt's
#                       ECAM bridge is never probed and the bus is still empty.
#   the recovery menu   no FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE. It is `default
#                       DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION`, so dropping DRM took it, while
#                       CONFIG_VT and DUMMY_CONSOLE stay on and keep /dev/tty1
#                       a writable character device with nothing behind it.
#                       Measured: /proc/consoles lists ttyAMA0 alone and
#                       /sys/class/graphics/fbcon does not exist. Recovery's
#                       text menu used to pick tty1 on the strength of the
#                       chardev test alone and drew into a dummy VT;
#                       ndsys-recovery.sh's recovery_tty() now asks for fbcon
#                       and falls through to /dev/console, which is where the
#                       text UI appears under QEMU until this symbol is back.
#                       nd-recui draws on /dev/fb0 and is unaffected.
#
# ============ THREE THINGS ADDED AFTER THE FIRST DRAFT, AND WHY ==========
#
# The first cut of this file left these out and listed them as known gaps. All
# three were measured on a booted guest before being added, because a symbol
# added on reasoning alone is how a config grows past the memory budget.
#
#   VFAT_FS + NLS_CODEPAGE_437 + NLS_ISO8859_1
#       NOT for the phone's own card -- that has been ONE EXT4 PARTITION since
#       0.5.0b (sdcard.sh:170: "FAT cannot record who owns a file"), and
#       EXT4_FS mounts it. A draft of run_qemu.sh warned on every boot that the
#       card was FAT32 and unmountable, which was wrong twice and has been
#       removed. These are here for the OTHER card: both defconfigs ship
#       dosfstools (mkfs.fat, fatlabel, fsck.fat), so a card somebody formatted
#       on a PC is a case the image is built to handle, and an emulator that
#       cannot open one is not emulating the phone.
#       Measured: +41 KB of zImage.
#
#   ZRAM + ZRAM_BACKEND_LZ4
#       neodct/overlay/etc/init.d/S16zram makes a 28 MB compressed swap and,
#       without the symbol, exits 0 in silence. That is the worst shape a gap
#       can take on this branch: the emulator quietly gets MORE headroom than
#       the phone, so a browser page that would be swapped on the phone is
#       merely resident here, and the memory pressure this whole target exists
#       to reproduce is the thing that goes missing.
#       Measured in the guest: `swapon /dev/zram0` gives 27,340k at priority
#       -2, and MemTotal moves 53,824 -> 53,820 kB. Four kilobytes for the
#       phone's swap behaviour.
#
#   BLK_DEV_LOOP
#       losetup is a declared initramfs applet with nothing to work on without
#       it. No script uses it today; it costs almost nothing and its absence is
#       the kind that is discovered at three in the morning during a recovery.
#
# Total for all six symbols: zImage 4,195,616 -> 4,264,656 bytes, MemTotal
# 53,824 -> 53,820 kB. The phone has ~54 MB, so the parity claim in this
# header still holds with them in.
#
# And the device-tree stage on top of that, measured the same way, on the same
# rootfs: zImage 4,264,656 -> 4,289,584 bytes and MemTotal 53,820 -> 53,824 kB
# with no -dtb, because EXPERT takes eleven HID drivers and DEBUG_MEMORY_INIT
# away as it goes. 54,812 kB is what a run_qemu.sh session sees, and the
# device-tree block above is where that 988 kB is accounted for.
#
# ============ THE CONFINEMENT SYMBOLS, AND WHY THE LIST MOVED ============
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TOUCHING NAMESPACES. This block used to say that NAMESPACES,
# USER_NS, UTS_NS, IPC_NS, PID_NS and NET_NS could not be spelled out below,
# because savedefconfig omits every symbol already at its default and the
# default was on. That was true and it is not true any more: EXPERT makes
# `default !EXPERT` mean n, so NAMESPACES is no longer at its default and
# savedefconfig now writes it out. It is in the body below, one line, and it
# has to stay there -- trap 4 is what happens when it goes.
#
# What did NOT change is the rest of the list. UTS_NS, IPC_NS, PID_NS and
# NET_NS are `default y` inside the NAMESPACES menu and SECCOMP is `default y`
# on this architecture, so all five are still absent from the body and still
# set in the expansion -- checked by running olddefconfig over this exact file
# and reading the result, not by reading the body. USER_NS is listed because
# it defaults to n, and it was listed before EXPERT landed too; what EXPERT
# changed is that the line above it now decides whether it survives.
# SECURITY-PLAN.md's Phase 2 and Phase 3 have what they need from this kernel.
#
# CONFIG_MNT_NS IS A DIFFERENT MATTER AND IS WORTH KNOWING. It does not exist:
# there is no `config MNT_NS` anywhere in the 6.12 tree. The CONFIG_MNT_NS=y
# line in the aarch64 file is discarded by olddefconfig and always was, and
# AGENTS.md names the same symbol by hand as the thing nd-selftest cannot check
# without. Mount namespaces are unconditional wherever NAMESPACES is set; the
# thing to look at on a running system is /proc/self/ns/mnt, and the thing to
# grep for in a kernel config is NAMESPACES.
#
# Free removals, and part of why parity was reached: SELINUX and the rest of
# the LSM/AUDIT machinery, ACPI, ARCH_VEXPRESS, SCSI/ATA, TCG_TPM and
# TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE. The SELinux block in the aarch64 file argues that a
# kernel option has to be present BEFORE the fleet grows because a kernel
# cannot ship in a .ndsw; that argument is about the PHONE's kernel, which is
# the SDK's 5.10 and is not this file.
#
# Two more facts about -M virt that are configuration questions and not kernel
# ones, recorded here because this is where somebody will look.
#
# ITS VIRTIO-MMIO BUS IS FORCE-LEGACY BY DEFAULT, so virtio_input refuses every
# device with a silent -ENODEV until QEMU is given
# `-global virtio-mmio.force-legacy=false`. EMPIRICAL-FINDINGS 10.
#
# AND IT HAS NO I2C CONTROLLER OF ITS OWN, which is why CONFIG_I2C_VIRTIO is
# here. It used to say "I2C_CHARDEV is set and /sys/class/i2c-dev is empty --
# the keypad stage needs vhost-user-i2c or a mock adapter", and that sentence
# is now out of date: the emulator has a real i2c bus. The bus is a
# `vhost-user-i2c-device` whose transfers are serviced OUTSIDE QEMU by
# neodct/tools/nd-i2c-keypadd, which models the phone's PCF8575 at 0x20 and
# its MAX17048 at 0x36; i2c-virtio binds to it, i2c_add_adapter() registers a
# real adapter, devtmpfs makes a real /dev/i2c-3 and nd_pcf8575.c's own
# ioctl(I2C_SLAVE) and two-byte write and read run against it.
#
# THE COST IS 560 BYTES, MEASURED, AND MemTotal DOES NOT MOVE. Two builds of
# this file on this kernel, differing only in this symbol:
#   zImage 4,289,584 -> 4,290,144, MemTotal 54,812 kB -> 54,812 kB.
# The other two candidates were both booted and are both dead: I2C_STUB is
# `depends on m` and its algorithm declares no .master_xfer, so every plain
# transfer is EOPNOTSUPP (measured errno=95 from this repo's own driver);
# I2C_GPIO on the pl061 costs 3,000 bytes, works, and can never have a chip on
# it because i2c-algo-bit has no reg_slave. nd-i2c-keypadd's header has the
# full argument.
CONFIG_SYSVIPC=y
CONFIG_POSIX_MQUEUE=y
